2. libjpeg (the IJG's licence), in package:twk_rich/image.dart

   lib/src/image/jpeg.dart translates the parts of libjpeg below into
   Dart, so that it decodes as `djpeg -dct int -nosmooth` does. They were
   compared with libjpeg-turbo 3.2.0
   (https://github.com/libjpeg-turbo/libjpeg-turbo/tree/3.2.0), whose
   files carry this code of the IJG's; the line numbers are that
   release's.

   _idct, the inverse DCT and dequantization of a block
     jpeg_idct_islow in src/jidctint.c (lines 102-143 and 190-436): the
     slow-but-accurate integer IDCT, after Loeffler, Ligtenberg and
     Moschytz — its 13-bit constants, two bits more kept between the
     passes, the columns first and the rows second, the same variables,
     order of operations and rounding, and the shortcut for a column or
     row whose AC terms are all zero.
     Changed: for 8-bit samples only, without the scaled IDCTs; the
     MULTIPLY, DEQUANTIZE and DESCALE macros written out; each result
     clamped to 0..255 after 128 is added, in place of the range-limit
     table, which gives the same samples unless corrupt coefficients take
     the sum beyond the table's range; the samples written into a plane
     at a stride rather than into rows.
     src/jidctint.c: Copyright (C) 1991-1998, Thomas G. Lane.
     Modification developed 2002-2018 by Guido Vollbeding. libjpeg-turbo
     Modifications: Copyright (C) 2015, 2020, 2022, 2026, D. R. Commander.

   _crToR, _cbToB, _crToG and _cbToG, the YCbCr to RGB tables
     build_ycc_rgb_table in src/jdcolor.c (lines 82-84 and 212-256; the
     same in src/jdmerge.c, lines 160-201): Cr to R and Cb to B rounded in
     advance, Cr to G and Cb to G kept with 16 bits of fraction and half
     added to Cb's. _Decoder._output adds them to Y as ycc_rgb_convert
     does in src/jdcolext.c (lines 55-64), and inverts the result for
     YCCK as ycck_cmyk_convert does in src/jdcolor.c (lines 436-445).
     Changed: the tables made once for 8-bit samples; a clamp to 0..255 in
     place of the range-limit table; the YCCK inks inverted after the
     clamp rather than before it, which gives the same values.
     src/jdcolor.c: Copyright (C) 1991-1997, Thomas G. Lane. Modified 2011
     by Guido Vollbeding. libjpeg-turbo Modifications: Copyright 2009
     Pierre Ossman <ossman@cendio.se> for Cendio AB; Copyright (C) 2009,
     2011-2012, 2014-2015, 2022, 2024-2025, D. R. Commander; Copyright
     (C) 2013, Linaro Limited.

   _Decoder._refineAc, a progressive scan's refinement of AC coefficients
     decode_mcu_AC_refine in src/jdphuff.c (lines 497-645): p1 and m1, the
     run of zero coefficients counted down while correction bits are
     appended to the non-zero ones, the newly non-zero coefficient placed
     where the run ends, and the end-of-band run.
     Changed: without input suspension, and so without undoing the newly
     non-zero coefficients; a coefficient of any size taken as one of
     size 1 without a warning; a coefficient past position 63 dropped;
     the restart handling and the bit reader the decoder's own.
     src/jdphuff.c: Copyright (C) 1995-1997, Thomas G. Lane. libjpeg-turbo
     Modifications: Copyright (C) 2015-2016, 2018-2022, D. R. Commander.

   _zigzag, the zigzag order of ITU-T T.81, ends with 16 entries of 63 as
   jpeg_natural_order does in src/jutils.c (lines 59-70), so that a run in
   corrupt data stays within its block.
   src/jutils.c: Copyright (C) 1991-1996, Thomas G. Lane. libjpeg-turbo
   Modifications: Copyright (C) 2022, D. R. Commander.

   The rest of the decoder is not taken from libjpeg's code: reading the
   markers, the Huffman tables and codes, the entropy-coded bits and
   restart markers, the scans (section 3), the colour space a file is in,
   chroma replicated to the full size, and CMYK to RGB.


3. jpgjs, as pdf.js (Apache License 2.0) and package:image (MIT License)
   keep it, in package:twk_rich/image.dart

   The way lib/src/image/jpeg.dart walks a scan and decodes the blocks of
   a baseline scan and of a progressive scan's first passes follows jpgjs,
   the JPEG decoder notmasteryet wrote and pdf.js took in, and its Dart
   port in package:image. They were compared with
     pdf.js v4.10.38, src/core/jpg.js
       (https://github.com/mozilla/pdf.js/blob/v4.10.38/src/core/jpg.js),
       cited below as jpg.js;
     package:image 4.5.4, lib/src/formats/jpeg/jpeg_scan.dart and
       jpeg_frame.dart
       (https://github.com/brendan-duncan/image/tree/4.5.4/lib/src/formats/jpeg),
       cited as jpeg_scan.dart and jpeg_frame.dart;
     and, where they came from, jpgjs's jpg.js at commit f1d3092
       (https://github.com/notmasteryet/jpgjs/blob/f1d30922fda93417669246f5a25cf2393dd9c108/jpg.js),
       cited as jpgjs.
   The line numbers are those versions'.

   _Decoder._scan, the walk through a scan
     The step that decodes a block chosen once a scan, from whether the
     file is progressive and the scan's spectral selection and successive
     approximation; a scan of one component taken block by block, the
     row mcu ~/ width and the column mcu % width; an interleaved scan
     taken MCU by MCU, each component's v × h blocks in turn at row
     mcuRow × v + j and column mcuColumn × h + i; the count of MCUs from
     either; the predictions and the end-of-band run reset at each
     restart.
     jpg.js lines 357-430 (decodeMcu, decodeBlock, the choice of
     decodeFn, mcuExpected and the loop over the MCUs); jpeg_scan.dart
     lines 52-108 and 325-350; jpgjs lines 342-404.

   _Decoder._block, modes 0 to 3, and _Decoder._extend
     The baseline block, the first DC scan, the DC refinement and the
     first AC scan: the DC difference as t == 0 ? 0 : extend(t) added to
     the component's prediction; rs split into s = rs & 15 and r = rs >> 4,
     a zero s ending the block (r < 15) or skipping 16 coefficients
     (r = 15); otherwise k += r and the coefficient at zigzag position k;
     in a first AC scan, an end-of-band run going on skipping the block,
     a new one of (1 << r) - 1 plus r bits, and each coefficient times
     1 << Al. _extend as receiveAndExtend.
     jpg.js lines 208-287 (receive, receiveAndExtend, decodeBaseline,
     decodeDCFirst, decodeDCSuccessive, decodeACFirst); jpeg_scan.dart
     lines 162-259; jpgjs lines 204-275.

   blocksPerLine, blocksPerColumn, _mcusPerLine and _mcusPerColumn
     The names of _Component's and _Decoder's counts of blocks and MCUs,
     as jpg.js lines 747-768 (prepareComponents) and jpeg_frame.dart
     lines 16-51 have them.

   The colour transform of three components
     An Adobe marker's transform, where there is one, outweighing any
     other sign: none where it says 0, or, without one, where the
     components are named R, G and B (0x52, 0x47, 0x42), as
     _isColorConversionNeeded in jpg.js lines 1232-1262.

   Changed: written as cases of one switch over the block's step rather
   than a function for each; Huffman codes decoded through lookup tables
   rather than a tree, and bits read up to 32 at a time, data cut short
   or ending at a marker reading as zero bits rather than failing;
   restart markers found by the decoder's own search; the counts named
   blocksPerLine and blocksPerColumn here are those padded to whole MCUs
   (jpgjs's blocksPerLineForMcu and blocksPerColumnForMcu), and a scan of
   one component takes ceil(ceil(X × h / hmax) / 8) blocks across, as
   T.81 A.1.1 and A.2.2 have it, where jpgjs takes ceil(ceil(X / 8) × h /
   hmax); the first DC scan shifts the prediction by Al rather than each
   difference, as libjpeg does; an end-of-band run's extra bits read only
   when r > 0; the R, G and B names counted only where there is no JFIF
   marker, as in libjpeg; the AC refinement libjpeg's (section 2), not
   jpgjs's.
